\title{TensorGuard: Sound Static Verification of \texttt{nn.Module}
  Shapes and Gradient Flow via a Refinement-Typed Calculus,\\
  with a Dynamo-Guard Correspondence and a Lean-Audited
  Operator-Rule Table}

\author{Anonymous Authors\\Submission under double-blind review}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\textbf{Thesis.} A small, sound, no-execution refinement-type
checker can verify shape and gradient-flow correctness of real
PyTorch \texttt{nn.Module} code that mainstream execution-based
tools (\texttt{torch.fx}+ShapeProp, \texttt{FakeTensorMode},
\texttt{torch.export}) cannot even \emph{run}, while reporting
\textsc{Abstain} as a first-class verdict on the precise
boundary where \textsc{TorchDynamo} would break the graph.
We instantiate this thesis as \textsc{TensorGuard}: a verifier
built around a refinement-type system in which a tensor type is
$\mathsf{Tensor}\{s,g\mid\varphi\}$ over symbolic shape $s$,
gradient-required flag $g$, and a Z3-decidable side condition
$\varphi$, with assume/guarantee composition at the
\texttt{nn.Module} class boundary and an autograd-aware backward
verifier. Refutations are reported under a three-way taxonomy
(\textsc{Refuted-Proof} / \textsc{Contract-Violation} /
\textsc{Library-Warn}); the soundness theorem covers exactly the
first two. The static refinements are placed in one-directional
correspondence with \textsc{TorchDynamo}'s runtime guards
(necessary, not sufficient; \Cref{thm:dynamo-corr}). On a
$488$-block real-source benchmark, \textsc{TensorGuard} is the
only tool with non-trivial coverage: the four PyTorch baselines
and Pytea are \textsc{N/A} on $\ge 435/488$ blocks (constructors
require \texttt{config} arguments no tracer can synthesise). On
a $60$-bug historical PyTorch shape corpus it issues
\textsc{Refuted-Proof} on $56/60$ ($93.3\%$) with $0$ abstains.
A hybrid TG$\to$\texttt{FakeTensor} fallback contributes
\emph{zero} additional resolutions on the same $488$ blocks: TG's
abstentions and the execution-based tools' inapplicability surface
coincide. A Lean~4 \emph{operator-rule audit} of $28$
shape-transfer rules ships sorry-free at the rule definitions and
agrees with \texttt{torch~2.9.1} on $28{,}000/28{,}000$ random
in-fragment cases; it does not mechanise the parser, the analyser
implementation, the assume/guarantee composition rule, or the
backward verifier (\Cref{tab:handler-soundness}).
\end{abstract}

